1
00:00:04,380 --> 00:00:06,280
I'm just gonna run to the store and get a few things.

2
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:07,330
I'll pick you up when you're done.

3
00:00:07,550 --> 00:00:08,130
Okay.

4
00:00:08,130 --> 00:00:10,670
I like it a little better when you stay, but all right.

5
00:00:11,790 --> 00:00:12,640
Hey, Sheldon.

6
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:15,720
Hello. I'm here for my haircut with Mr. D'Onofrio.

7
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:17,960
I'm sorry, Uncle Tony's in the hospital.

8
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:19,180
He's pretty sick.

9
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:22,480
Oh, dear, Mr. D'Onofrio's in the hospital.

10
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:25,090
Why do these things always happen to me?

11
00:00:26,050 --> 00:00:27,510
I can cut it for you.

12
00:00:27,570 --> 00:00:29,350
You're not Mr. D'Onofrio.

13
00:00:30,120 --> 00:00:32,320
I get my hair cut by Mr. D'Onofrio.

14
00:00:32,930 --> 00:00:34,230
You believe this guy?

15
00:00:35,270 --> 00:00:36,700
Excuse us for a second.

16
00:00:36,710 --> 00:00:38,470
Sheldon, it's okay, he can do it.

17
00:00:38,470 --> 00:00:39,330
He's a barber.

18
00:00:39,330 --> 00:00:42,190
He's not a barber, he's the nephew.

19
00:00:42,930 --> 00:00:44,360
He's an example of the kind of nepotism

20
00:00:44,360 --> 00:00:47,130
that runs rampant in the barbering industry.

21
00:00:47,430 --> 00:00:50,660
Besides, Mr. D'Onofrio knows exactly how I like my hair done

22
00:00:50,660 --> 00:00:54,300
because he has all my haircut records from my barber in Texas.

23
00:00:55,370 --> 00:00:56,660
What are you talking about?

24
00:00:56,660 --> 00:00:58,190
When I first moved here,

25
00:00:58,190 --> 00:01:00,040
I was nervous about finding a new barber,

26
00:01:00,040 --> 00:01:02,320
so my mother had all my haircut records

27
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:04,210
sent here to Mr. D'Onofrio.

28
00:01:05,270 --> 00:01:07,920
There's no such thing as haircut records.

29
00:01:09,020 --> 00:01:10,540
Yes, there are.

30
00:01:11,570 --> 00:01:12,740
Have you ever seen them?

31
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:15,160
No, but my mother assured me they were sent here,

32
00:01:15,160 --> 00:01:17,070
and I'll bet you dollars to donuts

33
00:01:17,070 --> 00:01:18,570
that this one doesn't have them.

34
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:20,160
Uh, excuse me.

35
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:23,010
Do you have access to my haircut records?

36
00:01:23,010 --> 00:01:24,200
Your what?

37
00:01:24,980 --> 00:01:26,640
To paraphrase T.S. Eliot,

38
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:28,120
this is the way the world ends--

39
00:01:28,120 --> 00:01:30,480
not with a bang but with a nephew.

40
00:01:32,630 --> 00:01:35,230
Sheldon, you're a grown man, he's a professional,

41
00:01:35,230 --> 00:01:39,330
and your haircut is number three on that poster from 1946.

42
00:01:39,920 --> 00:01:40,700
Just sit down

43
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:41,930
and let him do it.

44
00:01:41,930 --> 00:01:44,430
Fine, but if I come out of this looking like a dork,

45
00:01:44,430 --> 00:01:45,490
it's on you.

46
00:01:54,540 --> 00:01:56,190
So my kid said the funniest thing today.

47
00:01:56,210 --> 00:01:57,140
Nope.

48
00:02:02,280 --> 00:02:03,710
When you tell this story later,

49
00:02:03,710 --> 00:02:06,430
the word we usually use is "quirky."

50
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:34,130
Can you pass the Parmesan cheese?

51
00:02:34,140 --> 00:02:34,610
Sure.

52
00:02:43,920 --> 00:02:45,180
What are you doing?

53
00:02:45,180 --> 00:02:46,990
I'm trying to get the hair out of my eyes.

54
00:02:48,410 --> 00:02:51,130
Sheldon, you are one day late for your haircut.

55
00:02:51,130 --> 00:02:53,690
Thank you for captioning my nightmare.

56
00:02:55,950 --> 00:02:58,040
Lookie here, I got my travel orders.

57
00:03:00,060 --> 00:03:02,200
"Payload Specialist Howard Wolowitz is requested

58
00:03:02,200 --> 00:03:04,630
to report to the NASA Johnson Space Center,

59
00:03:04,630 --> 00:03:05,630
Houston, Texas,

60
00:03:05,630 --> 00:03:07,970
for astronaut training Monday 8:00 a.m."

61
00:03:07,970 --> 00:03:11,040
Yeah, but it's from NASA, so it's 0800.

62
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:12,540
But it says "8:00 a.m."

63
00:03:14,080 --> 00:03:16,440
You read it as "0800."

64
00:03:16,440 --> 00:03:18,030
It doesn't have an "O" in front of it.

65
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:21,300
You know what does have an "oh" in front of it?

66
00:03:21,300 --> 00:03:22,610
"Oh, my God, I'm an astronaut,

67
00:03:22,610 --> 00:03:23,960
and you're dying of jealousy."

68
00:03:26,290 --> 00:03:27,220
So, what kind of things are they...?

69
00:03:27,220 --> 00:03:28,770
Will you stop that?

70
00:03:30,520 --> 00:03:34,390
I can't help it, I feel like a teen heartthrob.

71
00:03:36,180 --> 00:03:37,880
You know, Sheldon, I used to cut my brother's hair.

72
00:03:37,880 --> 00:03:38,950
I could do it for you.

73
00:03:38,950 --> 00:03:41,840
Penny, I know you mean well,

74
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:44,880
offering the skills of the hill folk, but, um,

75
00:03:46,160 --> 00:03:46,970
here in town,

76
00:03:46,970 --> 00:03:48,530
we don't churn our own butter,

77
00:03:48,530 --> 00:03:50,670
we don't make dresses out of gunny sacks,

78
00:03:50,670 --> 00:03:53,010
and, uh, we sure as shootin' don't get our hair cut

79
00:03:53,010 --> 00:03:54,010
by bottle-blonde...

80
00:03:54,020 --> 00:03:55,670
Sheldon, be nice.

81
00:03:56,500 --> 00:03:58,690
I'm sorry, it's the bad boy attitude

82
00:03:58,690 --> 00:04:00,110
that comes with this hair.

83
00:04:03,640 --> 00:04:04,760
You could go to my guy.

84
00:04:04,760 --> 00:04:06,740
He's at Juan-Juan in Beverly Hills.

85
00:04:06,740 --> 00:04:08,920
They bring you a cup of tea, they'll massage your scalp.

86
00:04:08,920 --> 00:04:11,790
It's about $200, but sometimes you look in the next chair

87
00:04:11,790 --> 00:04:14,920
and you see a superstar like Tony Danza.

88
00:04:16,410 --> 00:04:17,600
Quick question here:

89
00:04:17,600 --> 00:04:19,360
Have we actually changed the conversation

90
00:04:19,360 --> 00:04:20,930
from "I'm going to astronaut training"

91
00:04:20,930 --> 00:04:23,000
to "Sheldon can't get a haircut"?

92
00:04:23,510 --> 00:04:25,080
Now who's dying of jealousy?

93
00:04:25,080 --> 00:04:27,030
"Oh," it's you.

94
00:04:30,650 --> 00:04:33,520
* I'm a cowboy *

95
00:04:33,520 --> 00:04:36,690
* On a steel horse I ride *

96
00:04:36,690 --> 00:04:38,530
* I'm wanted *

97
00:04:41,180 --> 00:04:43,160
* I'm wanted *

98
00:04:45,860 --> 00:04:47,570
* Wanted *

99
00:04:47,570 --> 00:04:50,350
* Dead or alive *

100
00:04:50,540 --> 00:04:51,200
Sheldon,

101
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:53,190
you're ruining girlfriend- boyfriend sing-along night.

102
00:04:54,630 --> 00:04:55,580
I'm sorry.

103
00:04:55,580 --> 00:04:58,650
I'm looking for a barber and I'm running out of time.

104
00:04:58,650 --> 00:04:59,830
My hair is growing at the rate

105
00:04:59,830 --> 00:05:02,920
of 4.6 yoctometers per femtosecond.

106
00:05:04,050 --> 00:05:06,750
I mean, if you're quiet, you can hear it.

107
00:05:08,970 --> 00:05:10,450
What about Supercuts?

108
00:05:10,450 --> 00:05:11,470
I tried once.

109
00:05:11,470 --> 00:05:12,790
They do men's and women's hair

110
00:05:12,790 --> 00:05:14,780
in the same room at the same time.

111
00:05:14,780 --> 00:05:17,350
It's like Sodom and Gomorrah with mousse.

112
00:05:19,180 --> 00:05:20,630
Well, this isn't a crisis.

113
00:05:20,630 --> 00:05:23,060
Why don't you just let your hair grow out a little?

114
00:05:23,180 --> 00:05:25,060
Why don't I let my hair grow out?

115
00:05:25,110 --> 00:05:27,730
Um, why don't I start wearing Birkenstocks

116
00:05:27,730 --> 00:05:30,830
and seeking validation of my opinions by asking

117
00:05:30,830 --> 00:05:32,440
"Can you dig it?"

118
00:05:33,750 --> 00:05:34,890
Well, I don't know.

119
00:05:34,890 --> 00:05:37,340
I think you might look sexy with long hair.

120
00:05:38,550 --> 00:05:41,110
The kind that flows down to your shoulders

121
00:05:41,110 --> 00:05:44,390
and blows back while riding on a horse.

122
00:05:46,590 --> 00:05:49,230
Bareback and bare-chested.

123
00:05:51,760 --> 00:05:54,450
I'm gonna go brush my teeth; it might take a while.

124
00:06:00,560 --> 00:06:02,030
Hi, sweetie.

125
00:06:02,030 --> 00:06:02,980
I miss you.

126
00:06:02,980 --> 00:06:04,680
I miss you, too.

127
00:06:04,680 --> 00:06:07,150
So tell me all about your first day.

128
00:06:07,150 --> 00:06:09,720
Oh, wow, where do I even start?

129
00:06:09,720 --> 00:06:13,020
I got to experience zero gravity.

130
00:06:13,020 --> 00:06:14,640
Cool. How do they do that?

131
00:06:14,640 --> 00:06:15,460
It's pretty neat.

132
00:06:15,460 --> 00:06:16,660
You get in this plane

133
00:06:16,660 --> 00:06:20,600
that goes almost straight up for, like, 20 seconds,

134
00:06:20,600 --> 00:06:25,270
and then straight back down like it's going to crash, and

135
00:06:26,050 --> 00:06:28,660
they do it over and over again, you know,

136
00:06:28,660 --> 00:06:30,560
no matter how many times you throw up.

137
00:06:31,290 --> 00:06:32,480
You threw up?

138
00:06:32,480 --> 00:06:34,740
Yeah...and the craziest part is,

139
00:06:34,740 --> 00:06:36,290
because there's no gravity,

140
00:06:36,290 --> 00:06:39,290
the throw-up kind of floats there...

141
00:06:41,110 --> 00:06:42,590
in a little ball,

142
00:06:42,940 --> 00:06:44,890
and if your mouth is open

143
00:06:45,120 --> 00:06:46,840
because you're screaming...

144
00:06:48,650 --> 00:06:52,800
...sometimes it just floats right back in.

145
00:06:55,020 --> 00:06:57,470
Boy, does everyone laugh at you when that happens.

146
00:06:58,230 --> 00:06:59,230
That sounds mean.

147
00:06:59,230 --> 00:07:00,390
No.

148
00:07:00,390 --> 00:07:02,000
Yeah, I would have laughed, too,

149
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:04,220
but I didn't want the vomit to come back out.

150
00:07:07,140 --> 00:07:08,330
Anyway...

151
00:07:09,110 --> 00:07:11,060
Oh, could you do me a favor

152
00:07:11,060 --> 00:07:14,170
and overnight me some more underwear?

153
00:07:14,170 --> 00:07:15,350
Sure, why?

154
00:07:15,350 --> 00:07:17,000
I got a look at the centrifuge

155
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:18,650
they're going to spin me around in tomorrow,

156
00:07:18,650 --> 00:07:21,470
and I have a hunch I packed a little light.

157
00:07:29,920 --> 00:07:31,660
Mr. D'Onofrio?

158
00:07:31,660 --> 00:07:33,380
It's me, Sheldon.

159
00:07:34,670 --> 00:07:38,250
They didn't have anything barber-themed in the gift shop,

160
00:07:38,250 --> 00:07:40,150
so I got you this.

161
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:42,140
I don't know if you can read his little T-shirt.

162
00:07:42,140 --> 00:07:45,250
It says, um, "Get well bear-y soon."

163
00:07:46,710 --> 00:07:48,950
Trust me, if you were even a little conscious right now,

164
00:07:48,950 --> 00:07:50,190
you'd be laughing.

165
00:07:53,650 --> 00:07:54,770
Anyway...

166
00:07:56,890 --> 00:08:00,200
There's new studies that show, people in comas

167
00:08:00,200 --> 00:08:03,020
are aware of everything going on around them.

168
00:08:03,160 --> 00:08:06,110
With that in mind, if you can hear me,

169
00:08:06,110 --> 00:08:08,060
move away from the light

170
00:08:08,060 --> 00:08:11,080
and toward the sound of these scissors.

171
00:08:12,940 --> 00:08:14,640
Can I help you?

172
00:08:14,640 --> 00:08:16,040
Yes.

173
00:08:16,040 --> 00:08:18,680
Do you have something I could use as a cape?

174
00:08:20,420 --> 00:08:22,710
Oh, dear, did we spit out our pills,

175
00:08:22,710 --> 00:08:23,920
slip out of the ninth floor,

176
00:08:23,920 --> 00:08:25,840
and go on a little adventure?

177
00:08:27,190 --> 00:08:28,500
Oh, no, no,

178
00:08:28,500 --> 00:08:31,010
I'm just here to get my hair cut.

179
00:08:34,790 --> 00:08:36,060
I-I see.

180
00:08:36,060 --> 00:08:38,350
J-Jus-Just wait here one moment.

181
00:08:38,830 --> 00:08:40,260
Security!

182
00:08:41,740 --> 00:08:43,210
I got to run.

183
00:08:43,500 --> 00:08:46,530
But not with scissors -- that would be unsafe.

184
00:08:52,510 --> 00:08:56,120
So, if I move my horsey here...

185
00:08:58,460 --> 00:09:00,960
...isn't that checkmate and I win?

186
00:09:11,410 --> 00:09:13,390
Well, is it or isn't it?

187
00:09:14,690 --> 00:09:16,870
You know, I think this is a good stopping point.

188
00:09:18,500 --> 00:09:20,130
Uh, it's your first real game.

189
00:09:20,130 --> 00:09:21,890
I threw a lot of information at you.

190
00:09:21,890 --> 00:09:23,190
Well, no,

191
00:09:23,190 --> 00:09:24,590
I mean, your king is trapped.

192
00:09:24,590 --> 00:09:27,160
He can't go here because of my lighthouse,

193
00:09:27,160 --> 00:09:28,340
and...

194
00:09:30,170 --> 00:09:33,530
he can't go here because of my pointy-head guy.

195
00:09:34,870 --> 00:09:37,400
Like I said, complicated game.

196
00:09:38,280 --> 00:09:40,120
So did I win or not?

197
00:09:40,120 --> 00:09:41,330
Did you have fun?

198
00:09:41,330 --> 00:09:43,300
Because if you had fun, then you are, you are a winner.

199
00:09:43,300 --> 00:09:45,380
That's, that-that's what chess is all about.

200
00:09:49,020 --> 00:09:50,930
- Sorry, Sheldon, I'll move.  - Eh, why?

201
00:09:50,930 --> 00:09:54,070
My spot, your spot-- what difference does it make?

202
00:09:58,070 --> 00:09:59,440
Okay, what just happened?

203
00:10:00,560 --> 00:10:01,910
I don't know.

204
00:10:01,910 --> 00:10:04,210
Between you playing chess like Bobby Fischer

205
00:10:04,210 --> 00:10:06,960
and Sheldon being okay with you in his spot,

206
00:10:06,960 --> 00:10:09,500
I'm guessing someone went back in time, stepped on a bug,

207
00:10:09,500 --> 00:10:11,690
and changed the course of human events.

208
00:10:13,060 --> 00:10:15,100
Uh, sweetie, are you all right?

209
00:10:15,500 --> 00:10:17,470
No, I'm not all right.

210
00:10:17,470 --> 00:10:20,630
It's been six days since I was supposed to get a haircut

211
00:10:20,630 --> 00:10:22,690
and nothing horrible has happened.

212
00:10:24,070 --> 00:10:25,920
Okay, I'm sorry, I don't understand.

213
00:10:26,230 --> 00:10:27,830
Leonard, explain it to her.

214
00:10:27,830 --> 00:10:30,020
Oh, uh, he's crazy.

215
00:10:32,120 --> 00:10:34,070
I have spent my whole life

216
00:10:34,070 --> 00:10:35,730
trying to bring order to the universe

217
00:10:35,730 --> 00:10:38,530
by carefully planning every moment of every day.

218
00:10:38,530 --> 00:10:39,780
But all my efforts--

219
00:10:39,780 --> 00:10:42,400
our dinner schedule, my pajama rotation,

220
00:10:42,400 --> 00:10:44,240
my bowel movement spreadsheet--

221
00:10:45,960 --> 00:10:47,140
it's clear now,

222
00:10:47,140 --> 00:10:48,570
I've been wasting my time.

223
00:10:49,550 --> 00:10:52,020
Good. I'm taking that disgusting chart off the fridge.

224
00:10:53,380 --> 00:10:55,760
You know, Sh-Sheldon,

225
00:10:55,760 --> 00:10:57,830
sometimes it's nice not knowing what's coming.

226
00:10:57,830 --> 00:10:58,990
I mean, look at me and Leonard.

227
00:10:58,990 --> 00:11:01,020
We went out, we broke up, now we're trying again.

228
00:11:01,020 --> 00:11:02,300
We don't know what's gonna happen.

229
00:11:02,300 --> 00:11:04,860
Oh, please, everyone knows what's going to happen.

230
00:11:07,290 --> 00:11:09,120
But I see your point.

231
00:11:09,120 --> 00:11:10,570
I think this could be good for you.

232
00:11:10,570 --> 00:11:13,180
Maybe it's time for you to shake things up a bit.

233
00:11:14,120 --> 00:11:15,360
You're right.

234
00:11:15,680 --> 00:11:17,920
I should embrace the chaos.

235
00:11:18,560 --> 00:11:19,620
Great.

236
00:11:19,620 --> 00:11:20,830
What are you going to do first?

237
00:11:20,830 --> 00:11:21,950
I don't know.

238
00:11:21,950 --> 00:11:23,480
I could do anything.

239
00:11:23,480 --> 00:11:25,110
All bets are off.

240
00:11:25,910 --> 00:11:27,770
The world is my oyster.

241
00:11:29,150 --> 00:11:30,590
I got it.

242
00:11:30,590 --> 00:11:33,780
I'm going to put on my Tuesday pajamas tonight.

243
00:11:38,250 --> 00:11:39,970
I got to tell you, I'm a little worried about him.

244
00:11:40,190 --> 00:11:42,540
If I were you, I'd be worried that a girl

245
00:11:42,540 --> 00:11:45,380
who's never played chess in her life just kicked your ass.

246
00:12:01,210 --> 00:12:03,390
Howie, what happened to you?

247
00:12:03,390 --> 00:12:07,270
We did overnight survival training in the wilderness.

248
00:12:07,910 --> 00:12:09,270
Big fun.

249
00:12:09,270 --> 00:12:11,060
Big, big fun.

250
00:12:12,190 --> 00:12:14,540
I was gonna freshen up for you

251
00:12:14,540 --> 00:12:18,410
but I blacked out a little on the way to the bathroom.

252
00:12:19,870 --> 00:12:20,890
Survival training?

253
00:12:20,890 --> 00:12:22,050
Is that like camping?

254
00:12:22,050 --> 00:12:23,090
Uh-huh.

255
00:12:23,090 --> 00:12:26,520
Except you don't have food or water,

256
00:12:26,750 --> 00:12:29,850
and they don't have a sunset Sabbath service

257
00:12:29,860 --> 00:12:32,130
like they do at Camp Hess-Kramer.

258
00:12:33,430 --> 00:12:34,960
Do you sleep in tents?

259
00:12:34,960 --> 00:12:36,080
No.

260
00:12:36,080 --> 00:12:38,240
I slept in a hole

261
00:12:38,510 --> 00:12:42,540
I dug in the ground with my bare hands.

262
00:12:43,670 --> 00:12:46,270
And at some point during the night,

263
00:12:46,270 --> 00:12:48,850
an armadillo crawled in

264
00:12:50,430 --> 00:12:52,350
and spooned me.

265
00:12:54,810 --> 00:12:56,190
Poor baby.

266
00:12:56,190 --> 00:12:57,280
But I did it.

267
00:12:57,280 --> 00:12:58,730
I survived.

268
